\begin{abstract}

Momentum and energy constraints couple the curvature, shear and tilt of a homogeneous spacetime before evolution is specified. For an oriented, expanding, locally rotationally symmetric Bianchi V slice containing one perfect fluid and a nonnegative cosmological constant, we give a complete parameterization of its instantaneous algebraically admissible region. A rationalized quadratic root yields the allowed rest-density interval while preserving the signed shear, fluid flux and anisotropic stress. The same construction gives a sharp tilt bound at fixed matter density and curvature, finite-tilt shear envelopes, and an ultrarelativistic supremum. We then exhibit two smooth collisionless Bianchi I metric histories with identical full final angular temperature functions and different instantaneous shear. Their endpoint shear amplitudes differ by a factor of $9/5$. This exact kinematic example identifies the history information lost by that radiation observation map. The results specify which deterministic images can be formed from the stated geometry and radiation assumptions, and which additional dynamical and observational inputs would connect those images.

\end{abstract}

\hypertarget{geometry-orientation-and-matter-variables}{%
\section{Geometry, orientation and matter variables}\label{geometry-orientation-and-matter-variables}}

Use signature \((-+++)\), proper time \(t\), and the LRS Bianchi V metric

\[
 ds^2=-c^2dt^2+X(t)^2dz^2
       +Y(t)^2e^{-2A_0z}(dx^2+dy^2).
 \tag{1}
\]

The coordinates and \(A_0>0\) are dimensionless; \(X,Y>0\) have length units. The orthonormal normal-frame axes are ordered \((e_x,e_y,e_z)\), with \(e_z\) along the oriented positive \(z\) direction. Set

\[
 H_X=\dot X/X,\qquad H_Y=\dot Y/Y,\qquad
 H=(H_X+2H_Y)/3>0,\qquad s=(H_X-H_Y)/3,
 \qquad z_s=s/H.
 \tag{2}
\]

Here \(H,s\) have inverse-time units. Mean expansion does not require every directional expansion rate to be positive. In particular, \(H_X=H(1+2z_s)\) may be negative. The shear and spatial curvature are

\[
 \frac{\sigma_{ij}}{H}=z_s\operatorname{diag}(-1,-1,2),
 \qquad \Sigma^2=\frac{\sigma:\sigma}{6H^2}=z_s^2,
 \qquad {}^{(3)}R=-6(A_0/X)^2,
 \qquad K=\left(\frac{cA_0}{XH}\right)^2>0.
 \tag{3}
\]

The curvature identity follows by setting \(r=Xz\) on a fixed-time slice: its warped spatial metric has sectional curvature \(-(A_0/X)^2\). The normal congruence is hypersurface orthogonal, so its vorticity vanishes. These statements refer to that congruence; the matter congruence is a separate object. {[}S1, S2, S4{]}

The perfect fluid has rest energy density \(\varepsilon\ge 0\), pressure \(p=w\varepsilon\), and signed velocity \(c\beta e_z\), where \(|\beta|<1\). Throughout the Bianchi V construction take

\[
 0\le w\le1,\qquad
 \gamma=(1-\beta^2)^{-1/2},\qquad
 \Omega=\frac{8\pi G\varepsilon}{3c^2H^2},\qquad
 \Omega_\Lambda=\frac{\Lambda c^2}{3H^2}\ge0.
 \tag{4}
\]

Thus \(\Omega\) normalizes \textbf{rest-frame energy} using the \textbf{normal-frame expansion rate}. It is not the fluid energy density measured by the normal observer. No additional matter component is included. Negative cosmological constant, \(w\) outside this interval, and multiple streams require a different domain calculation.

\hypertarget{a-complete-instantaneous-constraint-region}{%
\section{A complete instantaneous constraint region}\label{a-complete-instantaneous-constraint-region}}

The normal energy parameter is

\[
 \Omega_n=\Omega[(1+w)\gamma^2-w]
         =\Omega\frac{1+w\beta^2}{1-\beta^2}.
 \tag{5}
\]

For the orientation in Section 1 the momentum and Hamiltonian constraints read

\[
 2\sqrt K\,z_s=\frac{(1+w)\Omega\beta}{1-\beta^2},
 \qquad
 1=K+z_s^2+\Omega_n+\Omega_\Lambda.
 \tag{6}
\]

The sign in the first equation is retained. In the stated coordinates, the retained metric calculation gives \(G_{0z}=-6A_0s\) and \(T_{0z}=-cX(\varepsilon+p)\gamma^2\beta\). Their Einstein equation gives \(6(A_0/X)(s/c)=(8\pi G/c^4)(\varepsilon+p)\gamma^2\beta\), which fixes the factor two in (6). Squaring yields

\[
 \boxed{\displaystyle
 \Sigma^2=\frac{[(1+w)\Omega]^2\beta^2}
                    {4K(1-\beta^2)^2}.}
 \tag{7}
\]

Equations (5)--(7) are the retained LRS single-fluid relations {[}S1, S2{]}. An admissible image must satisfy their joint constraints; separately chosen bounds on the variables need not describe a realizable tuple.

For fixed \(K,\beta,w\), define

\[
 A=\frac{(1+w)^2\beta^2}{4K(1-\beta^2)^2},\qquad
 B=\frac{1+w\beta^2}{1-\beta^2},\qquad D=1-K.
 \tag{8}
\]

\textbf{Proposition 1.} There are no admissible tuples for \(K>1\). For \(0<K\le 1\), the entire algebraically admissible set is parameterized by

\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \Omega_{\max}&=\frac{2D}{B+\sqrt{B^2+4AD}},
 &\Omega&=u\Omega_{\max},\quad 0\le u\le1,\\
 z_s&=\frac{(1+w)\Omega\beta}{2\sqrt K(1-\beta^2)},
 &\Sigma^2&=A\Omega^2,\\
 \Omega_n&=B\Omega,
 &\Omega_\Lambda&=D-B\Omega-A\Omega^2.
 \end{aligned}
 \tag{9}
\]

\emph{Proof.} Substitution in (6) gives \(A\Omega^2+B\Omega\le D\). Here \(A\ge 0\) and \(B>0\), so \(f(\Omega)=A\Omega^2+B\Omega\) is strictly increasing for \(\Omega\ge 0\). For \(D<0\) no nonnegative \(\Omega\) satisfies the inequality. For \(D\ge 0\), the unique nonnegative solution of \(f(\Omega)=D\) is the rationalized root in (9). Its denominator is positive, including \(A=0\). Monotonicity gives exactly \(0\le \Omega\le \Omega_{max}\). Conversely (9) supplies a nonnegative \(\Omega_\Lambda\), the signed momentum relation, and the Hamiltonian relation for every parameter value in that interval. This proves necessary and sufficient instantaneous constraint compatibility in the stipulated sector.

The expression remains regular at \(\beta=0\), where \(A=0\), \(B=1\), \(\Omega_{max}=1-K\), and \(z_s=0\). At \(K=1\), \(\Omega_{max}=0\). If \(\Omega=0\), fluid stress and flux vanish and (6) forces \(z_s=0\); the coordinate label \(\beta\) then has no physical matter velocity to describe. It is recorded as undefined. The shear-free result at \(\beta=0\) belongs to this \(K>0\) LRS single-fluid constraint branch.

The parameterization also gives an instantaneous metric witness. Choose any \(H>0\), \(A_0>0\), and \(Y>0\), then take

\[
 \begin{gathered}
 X=\frac{cA_0}{H\sqrt K},\quad
 \dot X=H(1+2z_s)X,\quad \dot Y=H(1-z_s)Y,\\
 \varepsilon=\frac{3c^2H^2\Omega}{8\pi G},\quad
 p=w\varepsilon,\quad \Lambda=\frac{3H^2\Omega_\Lambda}{c^2}.
 \end{gathered}
 \tag{10}
\]

These values satisfy the two constraints on the specified slice. A time development would require the remaining Einstein and matter equations, with compatible initial and boundary conditions. The free chart parameters in (9) are not assumed to remain constant during such a development.

\hypertarget{the-joint-tensor-image}{%
\section{The joint tensor image}\label{the-joint-tensor-image}}

Let \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{crit}}=3c^2H^2/(8\pi G)\), and define

\[
 E_{\mathrm{tilt}}=\frac{(1+w)\Omega\beta^2}{1-\beta^2},\qquad
 f_z=\frac{(1+w)\Omega\beta}{1-\beta^2}=2\sqrt K\,z_s.
 \tag{11}
\]

Here \(f_z\) is the normal-frame energy-flux component divided by \(c\varepsilon_{\mathrm{crit}}\). Equivalently, it is momentum density divided by \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{crit}}/c\). The fluid's full normal-frame carrier is

\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \varepsilon_n/\varepsilon_{\mathrm{crit}}&=\Omega+E_{\mathrm{tilt}}=\Omega_n,
 & p_n/\varepsilon_{\mathrm{crit}}&=w\Omega+E_{\mathrm{tilt}}/3,\\
 \boldsymbol f&=(0,0,f_z),
 &\boldsymbol\beta&=(0,0,\beta),\\
 \pi_{ij}/\varepsilon_{\mathrm{crit}}&=
 E_{\mathrm{tilt}}\operatorname{diag}(-1/3,-1/3,2/3),
 &\sigma_{ij}/H&=z_s\operatorname{diag}(-1,-1,2).
 \end{aligned}
 \tag{12}
\]

The isotropic pressure and STF stress in (12) refer to the fluid; the cosmological constant is kept separately. Both tensors have vanishing trace, with \(\|\sigma/H\|_F^2=6\Sigma^2\) and \(\|\pi/\varepsilon_{\mathrm{crit}}\|_F^2=2E_{\mathrm{tilt}}^2/3\). The same axis and the same parameter tuple generate every entry.

At fixed \(K,\beta,w\), varying \(u\) in (9) yields the exact images

\[
 \Sigma^2\in[0,A\Omega_{\max}^2],\qquad
 \Omega_n\in[0,B\Omega_{\max}],\qquad
 \Omega_\Lambda\in[0,1-K].
 \tag{13}
\]

For \(\beta>0\), \(z_s\in[0,\sqrt A\Omega_{max}]\); for \(\beta<0\) the interval is \([-\sqrt A\Omega_{max},0]\). These are projections of one joint curve. Its cosmological-constant contribution decreases strictly with \(\Omega\), and the endpoint \(u=1\) has \(\Omega_\Lambda=0\). Combining arbitrary endpoints from the separate intervals would lose this dependence.

Under \(\beta\mapsto-\beta\), the signed shear and flux reverse while \(\Sigma^2\), density, pressure and anisotropic stress remain unchanged. A squared shear image therefore discards orientation even in this simple sector. The normal congruence still has exactly zero vorticity; matter-frame vorticity has not been determined by the instantaneous chart.

Figure 1 displays \(\Omega_{max}\) and its signed boundary shear for \(w=0,1/3\), \(0.02\le K\le 0.98\) and \(-0.9\le \beta\le 0.9\). Figure 2 retains the coupled image curves and their signed mirror branches. The deterministic parameter grids carry no probability or population weighting. Their tabulated full tuples retain the vectors and tensors in (12).

