\hypertarget{tilt-and-shear-bounds}{%
\section{Tilt and shear bounds}\label{tilt-and-shear-bounds}}

\hypertarget{fixed-matter-and-curvature}{%
\subsection{Fixed matter and curvature}\label{fixed-matter-and-curvature}}

For fixed \(K>0,\Omega>0,w\), with \(\Omega_\Lambda\ge 0\) free, put

\[
 C=(1+w)\Omega,\quad a=C^2/(4K),\quad b=C+a,\quad
 d=1-K-\Omega,\quad t=\sinh^2\chi=\frac{\beta^2}{1-\beta^2}.
 \tag{14}
\]

Equation (6) becomes

\[
 \Omega_n=\Omega+Ct,\qquad \Sigma^2=at(1+t),\qquad
 at^2+bt\le d.
 \tag{15}
\]

\textbf{Proposition 2.} The fixed-matter domain is empty when \(d<0\). For \(d\ge 0\), its exact speed interval is

\[
 0\le t\le t_{\max}=\frac{2d}{b+\sqrt{b^2+4ad}},
 \qquad
 |\beta|\le\sqrt{\frac{t_{\max}}{1+t_{\max}}}.
 \tag{16}
\]

\emph{Proof.} Both \(a\) and \(b\) are positive. The left side of (15) is strictly increasing on \(t\ge 0\), so the same monotone-root argument as in Proposition 1 applies. The transformation \(t\mapsto\sqrt{t/(1+t)}\) is increasing. At the upper endpoint \(\Omega_\Lambda=0\), while \(d=0\) permits only \(\beta=0\). If \(\Omega_\Lambda\) is fixed as well, replace \(d\) by \(1-K-\Omega-\Omega_\Lambda\) and impose equality in (15): when this value is nonnegative it selects a unique \(|\beta|\). The vacuum case uses the separate rule following (9).

Figure 3 shows (16) for \(\Omega=0.1,0.3,0.6\) and \(w=0,1/3\). Regions with \(K+\Omega>1\) are empty and have no numerical speed value. The horizontal display limit \(|\beta|=0.9\) is a plotting-domain choice, not a replacement for the exact bound.

A related necessary implication is useful when a common-frame shear ceiling and matter floor are independently available. For \(w>-1\), \(\Omega\ge \Omega_{min}>0\), \(K\le K_{max}\), and \(|z_s|\le \Sigma_{max}\), (6) gives

\[
 \frac{|\beta|}{1-\beta^2}\le a_0,
 \qquad a_0=\frac{2\sqrt{K_{\max}}\Sigma_{\max}}
                         {(1+w)\Omega_{\min}},
 \qquad
 |\beta|\le\frac{2a_0}{1+\sqrt{1+4a_0^2}}.
 \tag{17}
\]

This retained conservative implication {[}S1{]} presupposes a nonempty compatible joint domain. A radiation quadrupole does not provide \(\Sigma_{max}\) without a specified shear-history response.

