\label{fig:fixed_matter_tilt}
\end{figure}

\hypertarget{finite-tilt-and-ultrarelativistic-envelopes}{%
\subsection{Finite-tilt and ultrarelativistic envelopes}\label{finite-tilt-and-ultrarelativistic-envelopes}}

For \(\Omega>0\), write \(v=|\beta|\) and

\[
 r=\frac{|\boldsymbol f|}{\Omega_n}
   =\frac{(1+w)v}{1+wv^2}.
 \tag{18}
\]

For \(0\le w\le 1\) and \(0\le v<1\), \(0\le r<1\), since

\[
 1+wv^2-(1+w)v=(1-v)(1-wv)>0.
 \tag{19}
\]

The momentum and Hamiltonian constraints consequently imply

\[
 2\sqrt K\,|z_s|\le\Omega_n\le1-K-z_s^2,
 \qquad
 |z_s|\le1-\sqrt K,
 \qquad \Sigma^2\le(1-\sqrt K)^2.
 \tag{20}
\]

At \(0<K<1\), finite timelike matter with nonzero shear obeys a strict inequality in the last bound. Its boundary is the supremum approached as \(v\to1\), \(\Omega\to0\), and \(\Omega_n\) remains finite. For example, take \(\Omega_\Lambda=0\), choose the positive solution in (21) below, and set \(\Omega=\Omega_n/B\). Then \(r\to1\), \(|z_s|\to1-\sqrt K\), \(\Omega_n\to2\sqrt K(1-\sqrt K)\), and \(B\to\infty\). This limiting sequence is not a finite-\(\beta\) vacuum state; setting \(\Omega=0\) first gives zero fluid energy and zero shear. At \(K=1\) the zero-shear vacuum endpoint trivially attains (20).

For a compact support \(v\le v_{max}<1\), the sharper maximum at fixed \(K,w\) occurs at \(v=v_{max}\) and \(\Omega_\Lambda=0\). Indeed,

\[
 \frac{dr}{dv}=\frac{(1+w)(1-wv^2)}{(1+wv^2)^2}>0,
 \qquad
 z_{\max}=\frac{r(1-K)}{\sqrt{K+r^2(1-K)}+\sqrt K},
 \qquad \Sigma^2_{\max,\mathrm{compact}}=z_{\max}^2.
 \tag{21}
\]

The root follows from \(z^2+2\sqrt K z/r=1-K\) for \(r>0\), with continuous value \(z_{max}=0\) at \(r=0\). It equals \(\sqrt A\,\Omega_{max}\). At fixed \(K\), increasing \(r\) increases the root. Also \(\partial r/\partial w=v(1-v^2)/(1+wv^2)^2\ge 0\), so the envelope over all \(0\le w\le 1\) occurs at \(w=1\). Figure 4 compares compact envelopes at \(v_{max}=0.9\) with the strict ultrarelativistic supremum in (20).

\begin{figure}[tbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.94\linewidth]{figures/shear_envelope.pdf}
\caption{Sharp compact-support shear envelopes at $|\beta|\le0.9$ for $w=0,1/3,1$, compared with the ultrarelativistic supremum $(1-\sqrt K)^2$. For $0<K<1$ the dashed limiting curve is approached at finite normal energy and vanishing rest density as $|\beta|\to1$; it is not an attained finite-timelike state.}
\label{fig:shear_envelope}
\end{figure}

\hypertarget{identical-radiation-and-different-shear-histories}{%
\section{Identical radiation and different shear histories}\label{identical-radiation-and-different-shear-histories}}

The previous sections concern instantaneous Bianchi V constraints. To examine information loss in a radiation observable, use the separately specified collisionless kinematic Bianchi I metric class from {[}S3{]}:

\[
 ds^2=-c^2dt^2+a(t)^2\sum_i e^{2b_i(t)}(dx^i)^2,
 \qquad \sum_i b_i=0.
 \tag{22}
\]

Let \(t_i=0\), \(t_f=\Delta>0\), \(\tau=t/\Delta\), \(a(t)=a_i e^{t/\Delta}\), and \(H=1/\Delta\). Initially the normal-frame radiation is an isotropic Planck distribution at temperature \(T_i\), and \(b_i(0)=0\). There are no subsequent collisions, emission terms or observer boosts. Define the dimensionless STF tensor

\[
 L=\epsilon\operatorname{diag}(-1,-1,2),\qquad
 \epsilon=\frac1{50},\qquad \alpha=\frac45,
 \tag{23}
\]

and two deformation histories \(B=\operatorname{diag}(b_i)\):

\[
 B_A(\tau)=\tau L,\qquad
 B_B(\tau)=[(1-\alpha)\tau+\alpha\tau^2]L.
 \tag{24}
\]

They are smooth Lorentzian metrics with positive directional scale factors and common positive mean expansion. Their orthonormal shear tensors are

\[
 \frac{\sigma_A}{H}=L,\qquad
 \frac{\sigma_B}{H}=(1-\alpha+2\alpha\tau)L.
 \tag{25}
\]

Both have \(B(0)=0\), \(B(1)=L\), and \(\int_0^\Delta\sigma\,dt=L\), although \(B_A(1/2)=L/2\) and \(B_B(1/2)=3L/10\). At the final time,

\[
 \sigma_B(t_f)=\frac95\sigma_A(t_f),\qquad
 \Sigma_A^2=\frac1{2500},\qquad
 \Sigma_B^2=\frac{81}{62500},\qquad
 \frac{\Sigma_B^2}{\Sigma_A^2}=\frac{81}{25}.
 \tag{26}
\]

These histories have been specified kinematically. No Einstein equation or perfect-fluid matter evolution is assumed for them.

\textbf{Proposition 3.} The two histories give exactly the same full final angular temperature function, despite their different instantaneous shear:

\[
 \mathcal O[B,a](\boldsymbol n)
 =\frac{T(\boldsymbol n,t_f)}{T_0}
 =\left[\sum_i e^{2b_i(t_f)}n_i^2\right]^{-1/2}
 =\left[e^{-2\epsilon}(1-\mu^2)+e^{4\epsilon}\mu^2\right]^{-1/2},
 \quad T_0=T_i a_i/a(t_f),\quad \mu=n_z.
 \tag{27}
\]

\emph{Proof.} Spatial translation symmetry conserves each covariant spatial photon momentum. A photon with final normal-frame energy \(E\) and direction \(\boldsymbol n\) has spatial momenta proportional to \(a_f e^{b_i(t_f)}E n_i\). At emission \(b_i=0\), so

\[
 \frac{E_i}{E}=\frac{a_f}{a_i}
           \left[\sum_i e^{2b_i(t_f)}n_i^2\right]^{1/2}.
 \tag{28}
\]

Liouville transport preserves the phase-space occupation function of the initial Planck distribution. Its energy rescaling therefore gives (27) in each final direction. Both histories share their initial state, \(a_f/a_i\), and \(B(t_f)\), so the result agrees for every unit vector \(\boldsymbol n\). This is equality of the entire temperature function, without a small-anisotropy or quadrupole truncation. As direct checks, \(T/T_0=e^\epsilon\) in the equatorial plane and \(e^{-2\epsilon}\) on the symmetry axis.

Figure 5 displays the different shear and deformation histories together with their coincident final temperature functions. The analytic equality establishes noninjectivity of the specified kinematic observation map. It does not supply an equivalence between two Einstein-perfect-fluid Bianchi V solutions. That stronger problem needs a declared dynamical solution family and its radiation response.

The information needed to distinguish this pair can also be stated exactly. Their final full-tensor separation is

\[
 \left\|\frac{\sigma_B-\sigma_A}{H}\right\|_F
       =\alpha\sqrt6\,\epsilon.
 \tag{29}
\]

If the two predictions are surrounded by equal closed deterministic Frobenius-error balls of radius \(r_{\mathrm{err}}\), the balls are disjoint precisely when

\[
 2r_{\mathrm{err}}<\alpha\sqrt6\,\epsilon.
 \tag{30}
\]

At equality they touch; above it their intersection is nonempty. Figure 6 displays this geometric threshold. No probability law, confidence level or statistical power is assigned to these error balls. An intermediate-time temperature would also distinguish the pair because their deformation tensors then differ, but no such measurement is supplied here.

