\caption{Exact kinematic Bianchi I histories with equal final radiation. The signed $zz$ shear components and $b_{zz}$ histories differ, while their full final axisymmetric temperature functions coincide. The endpoint shear ratio is $9/5$. The radiation equality follows from common endpoint deformation and collisionless transport, without an Einstein-perfect-fluid evolution assumption.}
\label{fig:history_temperature}
\end{figure}
\begin{figure}[tbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.94\linewidth]{figures/error_geometry.pdf}
\caption{Deterministic separation of the history pair by an additional endpoint shear measurement. Equal closed Frobenius-error balls are disjoint for $r_{\mathrm{err}}<\alpha\sqrt6\epsilon/2$, touch at equality, and intersect above it. The radius is a deterministic tensor-error allowance with no probability or confidence interpretation.}
\label{fig:error_geometry}
\end{figure}

\hypertarget{physical-interpretation-and-scope}{%
\section{Physical interpretation and scope}\label{physical-interpretation-and-scope}}

The LRS Bianchi V calculation shows how curvature and momentum couple a signed shear to a rest-density and tilt state. Its allowed set is a joint instantaneous constraint image. The Bianchi I example shows that even a complete final temperature pattern can leave instantaneous shear nonunique under a specified kinematic radiation map. Connecting a measured sky to the Bianchi V bounds therefore requires a source-matched history response, together with its matter, propagation and observer assumptions.

All plotted images and error bounds in this note are deterministic. There is no empirical sky, mask, covariance, null ensemble, parameter probability law, likelihood or posterior. The only radiation transfer used is the explicit collisionless map (27); no native solver, external transfer calibration, mode atlas or polarization map is used. The calculation establishes neither a Bianchi family identification nor an all-\(m\) temperature/polarization prediction for the Bianchi V sector. An external atlas is unnecessary for the conditional algebraic claims proved here, while stronger history and identification claims retain their own missing inputs.

The tensors in (12) are defined carriers, and \(\Sigma^2\) is a geometric scalar within the stated normal frame. A signed \(x_C\) requires a specified same-frame comparator with explicit energy and curvature subtraction. A normalized \(Q\) score requires its anchor and denominator; \(F\) requires a support measure and appropriate ceiling; \(\Pi\) requires a joint probability law; and \(G_F\) requires supported depth-dependent \(F\) reports and their dependence. Those inputs are absent, so these quantities are not evaluated. The radiation quadrupole tensor, when mentioned as \(Q_T\), is distinct from the normalized \(Q\) score. Normal vorticity is exactly zero by hypersurface orthogonality; matter-frame vorticity remains undetermined.

\hypertarget{sources-and-reproducibility}{%
\section{Sources and reproducibility}\label{sources-and-reproducibility}}

S1 is the retained \texttt{physical\_derivations.md}, P9--P11; S2 is the retained \texttt{independent\_physical\_review.md}, ``Metric-level LRS V check and CMB scope''; S3 is the retained \texttt{legacy\_reconstruction\_chapter.md}, ``What the CMB quadrupole remembers'' and ``A conditional Bianchi-V consistency curve''; S4 is the retained \texttt{mathematical\_recovery.md}, ``Congruence, curvature contraction, and the signed comparator.'' Their paths and SHA-256 identities are given in \texttt{science/CLAIM\_SOURCE\_MAP.md}. The metric identities and exact radiation map are reused from these sources; Propositions 1--3 include the proofs needed for this note. Historical finite checks remain historical supporting evidence and are not represented as newly executed symbolic or four-axis verification.

The accompanying consumer verifies the frozen source identities, evaluates the explicit algebraic formulas, and writes full tensor tuples and plotting arrays. Tables and figures use binary64 and the exact finite grids stated in \texttt{science/BUILD\_CONTRACT.md}. Analytic proofs establish the full-domain results; generated residual checks establish faithful evaluation on the declared grids. No differential equation is numerically integrated.
